Query Middleware in Mongoose — Clear, Exact, and System-Level

In Mongoose, query middleware are hooks that run on the query object, before or after a query is executed against MongoDB.

They do not work with actual document data.
They work with query instructions.

Formal Definition

Query middleware intercepts and modifies database queries (find, update, delete) before or after they are executed, without loading documents into memory.

What this Refers To (Critical)

Inside query middleware:

this === Query object


Not a document.
Not actual data.

This means:

You can modify filters

You can modify options

You can inject constraints

But you cannot read document fields.

Query Middleware Lifecycle
Example
schema.pre("find", function () {
  this.where({ isDeleted: false });
});

Execution Flow

User.find() creates a query object

pre("find") runs

Query is modified

MongoDB executes the query

post("find") runs

Supported Query Middleware Hooks

Most common:

Operation	Hook
find	pre, post
findOne	pre, post
updateOne	pre, post
deleteOne	pre, post
findOneAndUpdate	pre, post
countDocuments	pre, post

Regex support:

schema.pre(/^find/, fn); // runs on find, findOne, findById

Real Production Example: Soft Delete
schema.pre(/^find/, function () {
  this.where({ deletedAt: null });
});

Why Query Middleware Is Required

Works on all queries

No document loading

Enforced globally

Cannot be bypassed accidentally

Example: Enforcing Tenant Isolation
schema.pre(/^find/, function () {
  this.where({ tenantId: this.getOptions().tenantId });
});

What Query Middleware CAN Do

Modify filters (this.where)

Add projections

Set options (limit, lean, hint)

Log queries

Enforce access rules

What Query Middleware CANNOT Do

Access document fields

Hash passwords

Validate schema fields

Call .save()

Use this.isModified()

Query vs Document Middleware (Direct Contrast)
Aspect	Query Middleware	Document Middleware
this	Query object	Document
Data available	Filters only	Full document
Runs on update queries	Yes	No
Validation	❌	✅
Field mutation	❌	✅
Common Mistake (Very Important)
schema.pre("updateOne", function () {
  this.password = hash("123"); // ❌ WRONG
});


Why?

No document exists

You do not know which documents are affected

This logic is unsafe

Correct Pattern When Updating Sensitive Fields
schema.pre("updateOne", function () {
  const update = this.getUpdate();
  if (update.password) {
    update.password = hash(update.password);
    this.setUpdate(update);
  }
});


Still dangerous. Best avoided.

Mental Model (Lock This In)

Query middleware = policy enforcement on database operations
Document middleware = data integrity enforcement on individual records